{"type": "vertex_text", "round": 1, "text": "I have completed a full source-fidelity and internal consistency review of the patent application draft. My process involved creating a complete source ledger from the inventor's disclosure and conversation files. I then systematically traced every limitation in every claim (1-17), every numbered part in the numeral list (10-86), and every structure described in the detailed description and figure briefs (FIGs. 1-7) back to an affirmative statement in the source ledger.\n\nThe review confirmed that all aspects of the draft are fully supported by the inventor's source material. This includes the core components (shell, tray, thermal module, lid), the registered passive circulation path, the lid-driven registration and retention mechanisms, and the two optional features (the condensate wick and the mechanical exposure indicator). The claims, description, and figure briefs are internally consistent in their use of terminology and reference numerals. The figure briefs correctly identify geometric representations as \"schematic\" where appropriate, and these representations do not add new, unsupported technical functions or relationships beyond what the inventor disclosed. No drafting notes, placeholders, or other unresolved issues were found in the text. The application is ready for filing from a source-fidelity and internal consistency standpoint.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Every claim limitation, numeral, numbered part, figure brief, drawing description, and affirmative inventor source was checked and traced without an unsupported technical assertion. The review included all claims (1-17), all numbered parts in the numeral list (10-86), the detailed description, and the briefs for all figures (1-7). Each feature, including the core components, the passive circulation path, the lid-driven registration mechanism, and the optional condensate wick and thermal indicator, was traced to an affirmative statement in the inventor's disclosure or conversation files. The use of 'schematic' in the figure briefs was found to be appropriate for depicting generic forms without adding unsupported technical substance, consistent with the shape-neutral language of the claims and description. The application text is internally consistent, with no remaining placeholders or unresolved issues.\",\n  \"findings\": []\n}\n```"}
